## an10.218
an10.218:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I don’t say that intentional deeds that have been performed and accumulated are eliminated without being experienced. | T: “Bhikkhus, I do not say that there is a termination of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated so long as one has not experienced its results,
an10.218:1.2 | S: And that may be in this very life, or in the next life, or in some subsequent period. | T: and that may be in this very life, or in the next rebirth, or on some subsequent occasion.
an10.218:1.3 | S: But in any case, there is no making an end of suffering without experiencing intentional deeds that have been performed and accumulated, I say. | T: But I do not say that there is making an end of suffering so long as one has not experienced the results of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated.
an10.218:2.1 | S: Now, there are three kinds of corruption and failure of bodily action that have unskillful intention, with suffering as their outcome and result. | T: “As to this, bhikkhus, there is a threefold corruption and failure of bodily kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition, having a painful outcome and result;
an10.218:2.2 | S: There are four kinds of corruption and failure of verbal action that have unskillful intention, with suffering as their outcome and result. | T: a fourfold corruption and failure of verbal kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition, having a painful outcome and result;
an10.218:2.3 | S: There are three kinds of corruption and failure of mental action that have unskillful intention, with suffering as their outcome and result. | T: and a threefold corruption and failure of mental kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition, having a painful outcome and result.
an10.218:3.1 | S: And what are the three kinds of corruption and failure of bodily action? … | T: (1)–(10) “And how, bhikkhus, is there a threefold corruption and failure of bodily kamma …
an10.218:3.2 | S: These are the three kinds of corruption and failure of bodily action. | T: (none)
an10.218:4.1 | S: And what are the four kinds of corruption and failure of verbal action? … | T: a fourfold corruption and failure of verbal kamma … …
an10.218:4.2 | S: These are the four kinds of corruption and failure of verbal action. | T: (none)
an10.218:5.1 | S: And what are the three kinds of corruption and failure of mental action? … | T: a threefold corruption and failure of mental kamma? …
an10.218:5.2 | S: These are the three kinds of corruption and failure of mental action. | T: all as in AN10.217 … having a painful outcome and result.
an10.218:6.1 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, sentient beings are reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell because of these three kinds of corruption and failure of bodily action, these four kinds of corruption and failure of verbal action, or these three kinds of corruption and failure of mental action that have unskillful intention, with suffering as their outcome and result. | T: “It is, bhikkhus, because of the threefold corruption and failure of bodily kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition … or it is because of the fourfold corruption and failure of verbal kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition … or it is because of the threefold corruption and failure of mental kamma, arisen from unwholesome volition, that with the breakup of the body, after death, beings are reborn in the plane of misery, in a bad destination, in the lower world, in hell.
an10.218:7.1 | S: I don’t say that intentional deeds that have been performed and accumulated are eliminated without being experienced. | T: “Bhikkhus, I do not say that there is a termination of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated so long as one has not experienced its results,
an10.218:7.2 | S: And that may be in this very life, or in the next life, or in some subsequent period. | T: and that may be in this very life, or in the next rebirth, or on some subsequent occasion.
an10.218:7.3 | S: But in any case, there is no making an end of suffering without experiencing intentional deeds that have been performed and accumulated, I say. | T: But I do not say that there is making an end of suffering so long as one has not experienced the results of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated.
an10.218:8.1 | S: Now, there are three kinds of successful bodily action that have skillful intention, with happiness as their outcome and result. | T: “As to this, bhikkhus, there is a threefold success of bodily kamma, arisen from wholesome volition, having a pleasant outcome and result.
an10.218:8.2 | S: There are four kinds of successful verbal action that have skillful intention, with happiness as their outcome and result. | T: There is a fourfold success of verbal kamma, arisen from wholesome volition, having a pleasant outcome and result.
an10.218:8.3 | S: There are three kinds of successful mental action that have skillful intention, with happiness as their outcome and result. | T: There is a threefold success of mental kamma, arisen from wholesome volition, having a pleasant outcome and result.
an10.218:9.1 | S: And what are the three kinds of successful bodily action? … | T: (1)–(10) “And how, bhikkhus, is there a threefold success of bodily kamma …
an10.218:9.2 | S: These are the three kinds of successful bodily action. | T: (none)
an10.218:10.1 | S: And what are the four kinds of successful verbal action? … | T: a fourfold success of verbal kamma … …
an10.218:10.2 | S: These are the four kinds of successful verbal action. | T: (none)
an10.218:11.1 | S: And what are the three kinds of successful mental action? … | T: a threefold success of mental kamma? …
an10.218:11.2 | S: These are the three kinds of successful mental action. | T: all as in AN10.217 … having a painful outcome and result.
an10.218:12.1 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, sentient beings are reborn in a good place, in heaven because of these three kinds of successful bodily action, these four kinds of successful verbal action, or these three kinds of successful mental action that have skillful intention, with happiness as their outcome and result. …” | T: “It is, bhikkhus, because of the threefold success of bodily kamma … or it is because of the fourfold success of verbal kamma … or it is because of the threefold success of mental kamma, arisen from wholesome volition, that with the breakup of the body, after death, beings are reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world. “Bhikkhus, I do not say that there is a termination of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated so long as one has not experienced its results, and that may be in this very life, or in the next rebirth, or on some subsequent occasion. But I do not say that there is making an end of suffering so long as one has not experienced the results of volitional kamma that has been done and accumulated.”
an10.218:12.2 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.64
sn22.64:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.64:1.2 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
sn22.64:1.3 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “Venerable sir, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief….”
sn22.64:1.4 | S: “When you conceive, mendicant, you’re bound by Māra. | T: “Bhikkhu, in conceiving one is bound by Mara;
sn22.64:1.5 | S: Not conceiving, you’re free from the Wicked One.” | T: by not conceiving one is freed from the Evil One.”
sn22.64:1.6 | S: “Understood, Blessed One! Understood, Holy One!” | T: “Understood, Blessed One! Understood, Fortunate One!”
sn22.64:2.1 | S: “But how do you see the detailed meaning of my brief statement?” | T: “In what way, bhikkhu, do you understand in detail the meaning of what was stated by me in brief?”
sn22.64:2.2 | S: “Sir, when you conceive form you’re bound by Māra. | T: “In conceiving form, venerable sir, one is bound by Mara;
sn22.64:2.3 | S: Not conceiving, you’re free from the Wicked One. | T: by not conceiving it one is freed from the Evil One.
sn22.64:2.4 | S: When you conceive feeling … | T: In conceiving feeling …
sn22.64:2.5 | S: perception … | T: perception …
sn22.64:2.6 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations …
sn22.64:2.7 | S: consciousness, you’re bound by Māra. | T: consciousness one is bound by Mara;
sn22.64:2.8 | S: Not conceiving, you’re free from the Wicked One. | T: by not conceiving it one is freed from the Evil One.
sn22.64:2.9 | S: That’s how I understand the detailed meaning of the Buddha’s brief statement.” | T: “It is in such a way, venerable sir, that I understand in detail the meaning of what was stated by the Blessed One in brief.”
sn22.64:3.1 | S: “Good, good, mendicant! | T: “Good, good, bhikkhu!
sn22.64:3.2 | S: It’s good that you understand the detailed meaning of what I’ve said in brief like this. | T: It is good that you understand in detail the meaning of what was stated by me in brief.
sn22.64:3.3 | S: When you conceive form you’re bound by Māra. | T: In conceiving form, bhikkhu, one is bound by Mara …
sn22.64:3.4 | S: Not conceiving, you’re free from the Wicked One. | T: (none)
sn22.64:3.5 | S: When you conceive feeling … | T: (none)
sn22.64:3.6 | S: perception … | T: as above in full …
sn22.64:3.7 | S: choices … | T: (none)
sn22.64:3.8 | S: consciousness, you’re bound by Māra. | T: (none)
sn22.64:3.9 | S: Not conceiving, you’re free from the Wicked One. | T: by not conceiving it one is freed from the Evil One.
sn22.64:3.10 | S: This is how to see the detailed meaning of what I said in brief.” … | T: It is in such a way that the meaning of what was stated by me in brief should be understood in detail.”
sn22.64:3.11 | S: And that mendicant became one of the perfected. | T: … And that bhikkhu became one of the arahants.
sn22.64:3.12 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

