## sn35.61
sn35.61:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the principle for depleting all fuel for grasping. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the Dhamma for the exhaustion of all clinging.
sn35.61:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.61:1.3 | S: And what is the principle for depleting all fuel for grasping? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the Dhamma for the exhaustion of all clinging?
sn35.61:1.4 | S: Eye consciousness arises dependent on the eye and sights. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: In dependence on the eye and forms, eye-consciousness arises….
sn35.61:1.5 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: as above …
sn35.61:1.6 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with the eye, sights, eye consciousness, eye contact, and feeling. | T: (none)
sn35.61:1.7 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they are liberated, they understand: ‘I have completely depleted fuel for grasping.’ | T: With its deliverance he understands: ‘Clinging has been exhausted by me.’
sn35.61:1.8 | S: Ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “In dependence on the ear and sounds …
sn35.61:1.9 | S: Mind consciousness arises dependent on the mind and ideas. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: the mind and mental phenomena, mind-consciousness arises….
sn35.61:1.10 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: …
sn35.61:1.11 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with the mind, ideas, mind consciousness, mind contact, and feeling. | T: (none)
sn35.61:1.12 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they are liberated, they understand: ‘I have completely depleted fuel for grasping.’ | T: With its deliverance he understands: ‘Clinging has been exhausted by me.’
sn35.61:1.13 | S: This is the principle for depleting all fuel for grasping.” | T: “This, bhikkhus, is the Dhamma for the exhaustion of all clinging.”
sn35.61:1.14 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.63
sn35.63:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn35.63:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Migajāla went up to the Buddha … | T: Then the Venerable Migajala approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side,
sn35.63:1.3 | S: and said to him: | T: and said to him:
sn35.63:1.4 | S: “Sir, they speak of one who lives alone. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘a lone dweller, a lone dweller.’
sn35.63:1.5 | S: How is one who lives alone defined? And how is living with a partner defined?” | T: In what way, venerable sir, is one a lone dweller, and in what way is one dwelling with a partner?”
sn35.63:2.1 | S: “Migajāla, there are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, Migajala, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.63:2.2 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them, welcomes them, and remains holding to them,
sn35.63:2.3 | S: this gives rise to relishing. | T: delight arises.
sn35.63:2.4 | S: When there’s relishing there’s lust. | T: When there is delight, there is infatuation.
sn35.63:2.5 | S: When there’s lust there is yoking. | T: When there is infatuation, there is bondage.
sn35.63:2.6 | S: A mendicant who is fettered by relishing is said to live with a partner. | T: Bound by the fetter of delight, Migajala, a bhikkhu is called one dwelling with a partner.
sn35.63:2.7 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, Migajala, sounds cognizable by the ear … odours cognizable by the nose … tastes cognizable by the tongue … tactile objects cognizable by the body …
sn35.63:2.8 | S: There are ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.63:2.9 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them …
sn35.63:2.10 | S: this gives rise to relishing. | T: (none)
sn35.63:2.11 | S: When there’s relishing there’s lust. | T: (none)
sn35.63:2.12 | S: When there’s lust there is yoking. | T: (none)
sn35.63:2.13 | S: A mendicant who is fettered by relishing is said to live with a partner. | T: he is called one dwelling with a partner.
sn35.63:2.14 | S: A mendicant who lives like this is said to live with a partner, even if they frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest that are quiet and still, far from the madding crowd, remote from human settlements, and fit for retreat. | T: “Migajala, even though a bhikkhu who dwells thus resorts to forests and groves, to remote lodgings where there are few sounds and little noise, desolate, hidden from people, appropriate for seclusion, he is still called one dwelling with a partner.
sn35.63:2.15 | P: atha kho sadutiyavihārīti vuccati. | T: (none)
sn35.63:2.16 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn35.63:2.17 | S: For craving is their partner, and they haven’t given it up. | T: Because craving is his partner, and he has not abandoned it;
sn35.63:2.18 | S: That’s why they’re said to live with a partner. | T: therefore he is called one dwelling with a partner.
sn35.63:3.1 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, Migajala, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.63:3.2 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them, does not welcome them, and does not remain holding to them,
sn35.63:3.3 | S: relishing ceases. | T: delight ceases.
sn35.63:3.4 | S: When there’s no relishing there’s no lust. | T: When there is no delight, there is no infatuation.
sn35.63:3.5 | S: When there’s no lust there’s no yoking. | T: When there is no infatuation, there is no bondage.
sn35.63:3.6 | S: A mendicant who is not fettered by relishing is said to live alone. | T: Released from the fetter of delight, Migajala, a bhikkhu is called a lone dweller.
sn35.63:3.7 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, Migajala, sounds cognizable by the ear … odours cognizable by the nose … tastes cognizable by the tongue … tactile objects cognizable by the body …
sn35.63:3.8 | S: There are ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.63:3.9 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them …
sn35.63:3.10 | S: relishing ceases. | T: (none)
sn35.63:3.11 | S: When there’s no relishing there’s no lust. | T: (none)
sn35.63:3.12 | S: When there’s no lust there’s no yoking. | T: (none)
sn35.63:3.13 | S: A mendicant who is not fettered by relishing is said to live alone. | T: he is called a lone dweller.
sn35.63:3.14 | S: A mendicant who lives like this is said to live alone, even if they live within a village crowded by monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen; by rulers and their chief ministers, and monastics of other religions and their disciples. | T: “Migajala, even though a bhikkhu who dwells thus lives in the vicinity of a village, associating with bhikkhus and bhikkhunis, with male and female lay followers, with kings and royal ministers, with sectarian teachers and their disciples,
sn35.63:3.15 | P: Atha kho ekavihārīti vuccati. | T: he is still called a lone dweller.
sn35.63:3.16 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn35.63:3.17 | S: For craving is their partner, and they have given it up. | T: Because craving is his partner, and he has abandoned it;
sn35.63:3.18 | S: That’s why they’re said to live alone.” | T: therefore he is called a lone dweller.”
